<!-- ==================== SECTION A ==================== -->
<h2>Section – A <span style="font-weight:400; font-size:1rem;">(10×1 = 10 marks) · answer any ten</span></h2>
<div class="section-note">✔️ All 12 questions shown; each 1‑mark concise answer.</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">1. State Pauli's exclusion principle. <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">No two electrons in an atom can have all four quantum numbers identical; an orbital can hold a maximum of two electrons with opposite spins.</div>
</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">2. Define Lattice energy. <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">The enthalpy change when 1 mole of an ionic solid is formed from its gaseous ions (or the energy released when gaseous ions combine to form a crystal).</div>
</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">3. Give an example for polar and non-polar molecule. <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">Polar: HCl (hydrogen chloride); Non-polar: Cl₂ (chlorine) or CH₄ (methane).</div>
</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">4. Define Electronegativity. <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">The ability of an atom in a molecule to attract shared electrons towards itself.</div>
</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">5. Define homolytic fission. <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">The breaking of a covalent bond such that each atom gets one of the shared electrons, forming free radicals.</div>
</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">6. What is optical activity? <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">The ability of a chiral compound to rotate the plane of plane‑polarised light.</div>
</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">7. What are diastereomers? <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">Stereoisomers that are not mirror images of each other; they have different physical and chemical properties.</div>
</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">8. What is sigma bond? <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">A covalent bond formed by the end-on (axial) overlap of atomic orbitals, with electron density concentrated along the internuclear axis.</div>
</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">9. Give the atomic refractivity of Carbon and Hydrogen. <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">Atomic refractivity: Carbon (C) = 2.418, Hydrogen (H) = 1.100 (based on specific systems; approximate values).</div>
</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">10. What is Bravais-lattice? <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">The 14 unique three-dimensional arrangements of points (unit cells) that describe the periodic structure of a crystal.</div>
</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">11. State law of corresponding states. <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">Gases at the same reduced temperature and reduced pressure have the same reduced volume and behave similarly.</div>
</div>

<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">12. Define mean free path. <span class="marks">1</span></div>
    <div class="answer">The average distance traveled by a gas molecule between successive collisions.</div>
</div>

<hr>

<!-- ==================== SECTION B ==================== -->
<h2>Section – B <span style="font-weight:400; font-size:1rem;">Inorganic · (2×10 = 20) · answer any two</span></h2>

<!-- Q13 -->
<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">13. (a) Define Electron Affinity? Discuss its variation in the periodic table. <span class="marks">6</span> &nbsp; (b) Write a note on physical significance of ψ and ψ². <span class="marks">4</span></div>
    <div class="answer">
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(a) – 6 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>Electron affinity is the energy released when an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom (X + e⁻ → X⁻ + energy).</li>
                <li><strong>Variation across a period (left to right):</strong> becomes more negative (increases) due to decreasing atomic size and increasing effective nuclear charge.</li>
                <li><strong>Variation down a group:</strong> generally becomes less negative (decreases) because of increased atomic size and shielding.</li>
                <li>Halogens have the highest electron affinity (most negative) in their periods.</li>
                <li>Noble gases have positive electron affinity (energy is required).</li>
                <li>It is related to electronegativity and non-metallic character.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(b) – 4 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>ψ (wave function) describes the amplitude of the electron wave; it can be positive or negative.</li>
                <li>ψ² gives the probability density of finding the electron in a given region of space.</li>
                <li>ψ² is always positive and represents the electron cloud (shape of the orbital).</li>
                <li>Higher ψ² means higher probability of finding the electron.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Q14 -->
<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">14. (a) What are the factors favoring the formation of ionic compounds? <span class="marks">6</span> &nbsp; (b) Write a note on effective nuclear charge. <span class="marks">4</span></div>
    <div class="answer">
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(a) – 6 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Low ionisation energy</strong> of the metal (cation formation is easier).</li>
                <li><strong>High electron affinity</strong> of the non-metal (anion formation is favourable).</li>
                <li><strong>Large electronegativity difference</strong> between the two atoms (typically > 1.7).</li>
                <li><strong>High lattice energy</strong> (strong electrostatic attraction between ions) stabilises the crystal.</li>
                <li><strong>Formation of stable octet</strong> (noble gas configuration) for both ions.</li>
                <li>These factors collectively make the overall formation of the ionic compound energetically favourable.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(b) – 4 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>Effective nuclear charge (Zₑff) is the net positive charge felt by an outer electron: Zₑff = Z – σ.</li>
                <li>Shielding constant (σ) accounts for the repulsion by inner electrons.</li>
                <li>Zₑff increases across a period (poor shielding by same shell) and decreases down a group.</li>
                <li>It explains trends in atomic size, ionisation energy, and electronegativity.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Q15 -->
<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">15. (a) Discuss the Born-Haber's cycle. <span class="marks">6</span> &nbsp; (b) State Hund's rule and Aufbau principle with example. <span class="marks">4</span></div>
    <div class="answer">
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(a) – 6 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>Born-Haber cycle is a thermodynamic cycle used to determine lattice energy of ionic compounds.</li>
                <li>It applies Hess's law to calculate lattice energy from other measurable quantities.</li>
                <li>Steps: sublimation of metal, ionisation energy, dissociation of diatomic gas, electron affinity, and formation enthalpy.</li>
                <li>For NaCl: ΔHf = ΔHsub + IE + ½BDE(Cl₂) + EA(Cl) + U (lattice energy).</li>
                <li>Lattice energy is calculated as: U = ΔHf – (ΔHsub + IE + ½BDE + EA).</li>
                <li>The cycle confirms that lattice energy is exothermic and very large for ionic solids.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(b) – 4 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Hund's rule:</strong> electrons occupy degenerate orbitals singly with parallel spins before pairing. Example: nitrogen (1s² 2s² 2p³) has three unpaired electrons in 2p orbitals.</li>
                <li><strong>Aufbau principle:</strong> electrons fill orbitals in order of increasing energy (lower energy first). Example: electron configuration of K (Z=19) is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s¹.</li>
                <li>Both rules help determine the electronic configuration of atoms.</li>
                <li>They are fundamental to understanding periodic trends.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="section-note">✔️ Answer any two of Q13, Q14, Q15 – all three shown.</div>

