<h2>Section – C <span style="font-weight:400; font-size:1rem;">Organic · (2×10 = 20) · answer any two</span></h2>

<!-- Q16 -->
<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">16. (a) What is hybridization? Discuss the SP hybridization by taking acetylene as an example. <span class="marks">6</span> &nbsp; (b) What are carbonium ions? Give one method of formation of carbonium ion. <span class="marks">4</span></div>
    <div class="answer">
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(a) – 6 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>Hybridisation is the mixing of atomic orbitals to form new equivalent hybrid orbitals.</li>
                <li>sp hybridisation involves one s and one p orbital forming two sp hybrid orbitals (linear, 180°).</li>
                <li>In acetylene (C₂H₂), each carbon uses two sp orbitals: one for σ bond with H, one for σ bond with other C.</li>
                <li>The two unhybridised p orbitals on each carbon overlap sideways to form two π bonds.</li>
                <li>This results in a C≡C triple bond (one σ, two π) and a linear molecular structure.</li>
                <li>Acetylene has a bond angle of 180°.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(b) – 4 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>Carbonium ions (carbocations) are trivalent carbon species with a positive charge (e.g., tert‑butyl cation).</li>
                <li>Formation by heterolytic cleavage of a C–X bond: R–X → R⁺ + X⁻.</li>
                <li>Protonation of an alkene followed by loss of a leaving group also forms carbocations.</li>
                <li>Stability order: 3° > 2° > 1° > methyl.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Q17 -->
<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">17. (a) Define with example of the following: (i) Elimination reaction. (ii) Hydrolysis. <span class="marks">6</span> &nbsp; (b) Write a note on enantiomers. <span class="marks">4</span></div>
    <div class="answer">
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(a) – 6 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Elimination reaction:</strong> removal of atoms/groups from adjacent carbons to form a multiple bond. Example: dehydration of ethanol (C₂H₅OH → C₂H₄ + H₂O).</li>
                <li>Example: dehydrohalogenation of bromoethane (CH₃CH₂Br + KOH → C₂H₄ + KBr + H₂O).</li>
                <li><strong>Hydrolysis:</strong> breaking of a bond using water. Example: hydrolysis of ester (CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O → CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH).</li>
                <li>Example: hydrolysis of amide (CH₃CONH₂ + H₂O → CH₃COOH + NH₃).</li>
                <li>Both are important functional group transformations.</li>
                <li>These reactions are widely used in organic synthesis.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(b) – 4 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>Enantiomers are stereoisomers that are non‑superimposable mirror images of each other.</li>
                <li>They contain a chiral centre (asymmetric carbon atom).</li>
                <li>They rotate plane‑polarised light in opposite directions (dextrorotatory and levorotatory).</li>
                <li>Example: (+) and (–) lactic acid (CH₃CH(OH)COOH).</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Q18 -->
<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">18. (a) What is Geometrical isomerism? Explain geometrical isomerism in maleic and fumaric acids. <span class="marks">6</span> &nbsp; (b) Define Bond length and Bond energy with example. <span class="marks">4</span></div>
    <div class="answer">
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(a) – 6 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>Geometrical (cis-trans) isomerism arises due to restricted rotation around a double bond or in ring compounds.</li>
                <li>Maleic acid is the cis isomer: both –COOH groups on the same side of the double bond.</li>
                <li>Maleic acid forms intramolecular H‑bonding, has mp 130°C, and is more water soluble.</li>
                <li>Fumaric acid is the trans isomer: –COOH groups on opposite sides.</li>
                <li>Fumaric acid has no intramolecular H‑bonding, higher mp (287°C), and is more stable.</li>
                <li>Interconversion requires breaking the π bond (heat/UV).</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(b) – 4 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Bond length:</strong> equilibrium distance between nuclei of bonded atoms (in Å). Example: C–C = 1.54 Å.</li>
                <li><strong>Bond energy:</strong> energy required to break 1 mole of a bond (in kJ/mol). Example: C–C = 347 kJ/mol.</li>
                <li>Shorter bond length = stronger bond (inverse relationship).</li>
                <li>Order: C≡C (1.20 Å, 839 kJ/mol) > C=C (1.34 Å, 611 kJ/mol) > C–C.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
