<h2>Section – D <span style="font-weight:400; font-size:1rem;">Physical · (2×10 = 20) · answer any two</span></h2>

<!-- Q19 -->
<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">19. (a) Discuss the isotherms of CO₂ and hence define the critical constants. <span class="marks">6</span> &nbsp; (b) Explain elements of symmetry. <span class="marks">4</span></div>
    <div class="answer">
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(a) – 6 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>Andrews studied isotherms of CO₂ at different temperatures.</li>
                <li>Below Tc (31.1°C), CO₂ liquefies under pressure; horizontal portion shows liquid‑gas equilibrium.</li>
                <li>At critical point, liquid and gas become indistinguishable.</li>
                <li><strong>Critical temperature (Tc):</strong> temp above which gas cannot be liquefied.</li>
                <li><strong>Critical pressure (Pc):</strong> pressure at critical point.</li>
                <li><strong>Critical volume (Vc):</strong> molar volume at critical point. For CO₂: Tc = 31.1°C, Pc = 73 atm.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(b) – 4 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Centre of symmetry (i):</strong> if (x,y,z) is present, then (-x,-y,-z) is also present.</li>
                <li><strong>Plane of symmetry (σ):</strong> a mirror plane that reflects one part onto the other.</li>
                <li><strong>Axis of symmetry (Cₙ):</strong> rotation by 360°/n gives identical arrangement.</li>
                <li>These symmetry elements are used to classify molecules and crystals.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Q20 -->
<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">20. (a) Discuss the application of parachor in elucidating the structure of quinone. <span class="marks">6</span> &nbsp; (b) Write a note on Maxwell's distribution of molecular velocities. <span class="marks">4</span></div>
    <div class="answer">
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(a) – 6 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>Parachor (P) = M γ^(1/4) / (ρ₁ – ρ₂), related to molar volume and surface tension.</li>
                <li>It is an additive and constitutive property (depends on structure).</li>
                <li>Quinone (C₆H₄O₂) has two possible structures: para‑ and ortho‑benzoquinone.</li>
                <li>By calculating parachor for each structure (using atomic and group contributions) and comparing with experimental parachor, the correct structure is identified.</li>
                <li>The experimental parachor matches the para‑benzoquinone structure.</li>
                <li>Thus, parachor is useful for structure elucidation.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(b) – 4 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>Maxwell‑Boltzmann distribution gives the distribution of speeds of gas molecules at a given temperature.</li>
                <li>It is a probability distribution: most molecules have speeds near the most probable speed (u_mp).</li>
                <li>The curve is asymmetric: rises sharply and tails off gradually.</li>
                <li>At higher temperatures, the curve broadens and shifts to higher speeds (u_avg, u_rms).</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Q21 -->
<div class="q-block">
    <div class="q">21. (a) Derive Bragg's equation. <span class="marks">6</span> &nbsp; (b) Calculate the parachor of benzene given surface tension of benzene at 20°C is 29.2 dynes/cm. Given the density of benzene at 20°C is 0.875 g/cc. <span class="marks">4</span></div>
    <div class="answer">
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(a) – 6 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>X‑rays are scattered by crystal planes (atomic layers).</li>
                <li>Constructive interference occurs when path difference = nλ.</li>
                <li>Path difference between waves from adjacent planes = 2d sinθ.</li>
                <li>Bragg's equation: nλ = 2d sinθ, where n = integer (order), d = interplanar spacing, θ = glancing angle.</li>
                <li>This equation is fundamental for X‑ray diffraction (XRD).</li>
                <li>It is used to determine crystal structures and d‑spacings.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-part"><span class="label">(b) – 4 marks</span>
            <ul>
                <li>Parachor (P) = M γ^(1/4) / (ρ₁ – ρ₂). For a liquid, P = M γ^(1/4) / ρ (since ρ₂ (vapour) ≈ 0).</li>
                <li>Molar mass of benzene (C₆H₆) = 78 g/mol.</li>
                <li>γ = 29.2 dynes/cm = 29.2 erg/cm² (since 1 dyne/cm = 1 erg/cm²).</li>
                <li>P = 78 × (29.2)^(1/4) / 0.875. (29.2)^(1/4) = (29.2)^0.25 ≈ 2.32.</li>
                <li>Numerator = 78 × 2.32 = 180.96. P = 180.96 / 0.875 ≈ <strong>206.8</strong> (approx).</li>
                <li>Parachor of benzene is approximately 206.8.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
